AWS-006 — Workflow State Model

Document ID: AWS-006
Title: Workflow State Model
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Workflow State Model (CWSM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Workflow State Model establishes the formal state machine governing workflow execution, including states, transitions, guards, triggers, actions, nested execution, parallel execution, terminal states, and execution invariants.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

State architecture
State semantics
Transition semantics
Triggers
Guards
Entry actions
Exit actions
Nested workflows
Parallel execution
Terminal behavior

This specification applies to every Workflow Instance.

3. Canonical Definition

A Workflow State is a formally defined execution condition representing the current operational status of a Workflow Instance at a specific point in time.

A state represents execution status.

It SHALL NOT represent workflow definition.

4. Objectives

The Workflow State Model SHALL:

Define deterministic execution.
Eliminate ambiguous behavior.
Support formal verification.
Enable monitoring.
Support recovery.
Preserve traceability.
Enable orchestration.
Support interoperability.
5. State Principles

Every workflow state SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Explicit State

Every Workflow Instance SHALL always occupy one explicitly defined state.

Implicit states SHALL NOT exist.

Deterministic Transition

Equivalent states receiving equivalent triggers SHALL produce equivalent transitions.

State Integrity

Only permitted transitions SHALL occur.

Immutable History

Historical states SHALL remain permanently recorded.

Explainability

Every transition SHALL include an explicit cause.

Recoverability

State recovery SHALL preserve execution consistency.

6. Canonical Workflow States

Atlas defines the following canonical states.

State	Description
Initialized	Workflow instance created
Ready	Awaiting execution
Running	Activities actively executing
Waiting	Awaiting event, dependency, or participant
Suspended	Execution paused
Recovering	Failure recovery in progress
Completed	Successfully finished
Failed	Unrecoverable execution failure
Cancelled	Intentionally terminated

Profiles MAY extend these states while preserving canonical semantics.

7. State Transition Model

Canonical execution flow:

Initialized
      │
      ▼
Ready
      │
      ▼
Running
      │
 ┌────┼───────────────┐
 ▼    ▼               ▼
Waiting Suspended Recovering
      │        │          │
      └────────┼──────────┘
               ▼
            Running
               │
        ┌──────┼────────┐
        ▼               ▼
Completed         Failed
        │               │
        └──────┬────────┘
               ▼
           Archived

All transitions SHALL be explicitly defined.

8. Transition Semantics

Every transition SHALL define:

Property	Description
Transition ID	Unique identifier
Source State	Origin
Target State	Destination
Trigger	Activating event
Guard	Required condition
Entry Actions	Actions on entering target
Exit Actions	Actions before leaving source
Generated Events	Produced Canonical Events

Transitions SHALL remain deterministic.

9. Triggers

Atlas defines canonical trigger categories.

Trigger	Description
Manual	Human initiated
Event	Canonical Event
Time	Time-based activation
Policy	Policy decision
System	Runtime signal
External	External integration
AI	Authorized AI decision

Triggers SHALL be independently traceable.

10. Guard Conditions

Every transition MAY define one or more guards.

Examples include:

Required approvals
Input validation
Resource availability
Policy authorization
Time constraints
Dependency completion
Security verification

Transitions SHALL NOT occur while mandatory guards remain unsatisfied.

11. Entry and Exit Actions

Every state MAY define:

Entry Actions

Executed immediately after entering a state.

Examples:

Allocate resources
Notify participants
Start timers
Generate events
Exit Actions

Executed immediately before leaving a state.

Examples:

Persist outputs
Release resources
Stop timers
Record metrics

Entry and exit actions SHALL preserve workflow semantics.

12. Nested States

Atlas supports hierarchical state machines.

Example:

Running
│
├── Review
│     ├── Assigned
│     ├── Reviewing
│     └── Approved
│
└── Validation
      ├── Executing
      └── Passed

Nested states SHALL preserve parent state semantics.

13. Parallel States

Atlas supports parallel execution regions.

Example:

Running
│
├── Structural Review
│
├── Cost Review
│
└── Safety Review

Parallel regions SHALL define synchronization conditions before continuation.

14. Terminal States

Atlas defines three terminal states.

State	Meaning
Completed	Successful completion
Failed	Unrecoverable failure
Cancelled	Intentional termination

Terminal states SHALL NOT transition to non-terminal states except through governed recovery mechanisms.

15. State Invariants

Every state SHALL define invariant conditions that remain true throughout its duration.

Examples:

State	Invariant
Running	At least one executable activity exists
Waiting	Awaiting a defined trigger or dependency
Suspended	No active execution occurs
Completed	All mandatory completion conditions satisfied

Invariant violations SHALL generate Canonical Events.

16. State Governance

Every state SHALL define:

Responsible authority
Applicable policies
Audit requirements
Recovery permissions
Monitoring requirements

Governance SHALL remain active throughout state execution.

17. Quality Requirements

Every Workflow State Model SHALL satisfy:

Determinism
Completeness
Explainability
Recoverability
Traceability
Auditability
Formal consistency
Governance compliance

Quality SHALL be independently verifiable.

18. Conformance

A Workflow State Model implementation conforms if it:

Implements canonical states.
Supports deterministic transitions.
Enforces guard conditions.
Supports entry and exit actions.
Supports nested and parallel states.
Preserves immutable state history.
Maintains governance metadata.
19. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-driven adaptive state transitions.
Probabilistic state analysis.
Distributed state synchronization.
Formal state verification.
Self-healing state recovery.
Digital Twin state alignment.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical state semantics.

